A pair of functional needle-nose pliers 3D-printed in rigid PLA components and a flexible TPU spring connected through hand pressed dovetail joints that can grip something as small as a through-hole resistor without using conventional metal springs, pins, or fasteners.

---

## The Challenge

Traditional pliers use a mechanical pivot and often a separate spring to control their motion. For this project, the goal was to create a functional tool using 3D-printed components while incorporating a flexible element that automatically returns the jaws to their open position.

The design needed to:

- Grip and pick up small through-hole resistors.
- Return to an open position when the handles are released.
- Use a flexible material to create the restoring force.
- Be assembled by hand without tools.
- Be printable using the available Voron 3D printers.

Rather than relying on a complicated multi-material print, I designed a modular system in which rigid and flexible components could be printed separately and joined together.

The result is a tool that demonstrates how material selection, mechanical design, and additive manufacturing can work together to produce a functional mechanism.

