## How It Works

The pliers operate through the interaction of rigid components and an elastic spring. Each material has a distinct mechanical purpose: the PLA maintains the geometry of the handles and gripping jaws, while the TPU deforms as the handles are squeezed and provides the restoring force when the applied force is removed.

![Exploded view of the pliers](images/exploded-view.png)

### 1. Rigid PLA Components

The four PLA components form the structural and gripping portions of the pliers. PLA was selected for the rigid parts because it is relatively stiff, easy to fabricate using FDM 3D printing, and suitable for maintaining the shape of the long, narrow jaws.

The approximately 220 mm overall length provides reach, while the narrow jaws allow the tool to access small electronic components.

The rigid components must withstand the force applied by the user without excessive deformation. They also need to maintain alignment so that the two gripping surfaces can contact a small resistor securely.

### 2. Flexible TPU Return Spring

The return spring is a square TPU component measuring approximately 45 × 45 × 10 mm. It uses TPU with a Shore hardness of 90A and a rectilinear infill pattern.

When the handles are squeezed, the spring deforms. Upon release, the elastic deformation produces a restoring force that moves the pliers back toward their open position.

Unlike a conventional metal coil spring, this component uses the flexibility of a printed polymer structure to produce the return motion.

The spring's geometry and material properties work together to determine how far the handles can move, how much force is needed to close the jaws, and how effectively the tool reopens.

A thicker or more constrained spring structure generally resists deformation more strongly, while a more compliant structure can allow greater movement. However, the actual response depends on the geometry, infill, print orientation, and material behavior.

### 3. Hand-Pressed Dovetail Connections

The TPU spring connects to the PLA components through dovetail joints that can be assembled by hand.

The dovetail geometry mechanically retains the flexible component without requiring screws, adhesives, or additional tools. The connection is designed to resist separation while allowing the spring to deform during operation.

This modular approach also allows the PLA components and TPU spring to be printed separately on the available Voron printers.

Separating the materials makes fabrication more practical and allows the spring geometry to be modified or reprinted without redesigning the entire tool.

The connection must balance two competing requirements: it needs enough clearance for hand assembly, but it must also remain secure during repeated opening and closing.

