## Design Process

The pliers were developed through multiple CAD and printing iterations. Instead of assuming that the initial geometry would work perfectly, I used physical prototypes to evaluate the fit, motion, and functionality of the design.

### Iteration 1 — Establishing the Geometry

The initial design focused on establishing the overall shape of the pliers, the arrangement of the rigid components, and the location of the flexible spring.

The first pliers were way too large on the first iteration. The jaw geometry was developed incorrectly, starting from a closed jaw position that required the user to operate the pliers using two hands.

### Iteration 2 — Spring and Connection Development

![Spring and dovetail prototype](images/iteration-2.jpg)

The next stage focused on opening the jaw and making the handles smaller.

In this iteration, the handles had to overlap in order to fully close the jaws, because the handles were still too bulky. 

### Final Design — A Functional Tool

![Final assembled pliers](images/final-pliers.jpg)

The final design had more narrow, pointed handles and slightly skinnier jaws to ensure that the jaws would fully close without handle overlap.

The completed tool successfully grips and picks up through-hole resistors, meeting the project's central functional objective.

---

### Assembly

The final assembly consists of four rigid PLA components and one flexible TPU spring.

1. Print the rigid PLA components and TPU spring separately.
2. Inspect the dovetail interfaces for dimensional accuracy and printing defects.
3. Align the dovetail features and hand-press the spring into the corresponding PLA components.
4. Check that all connections are fully seated and that the spring is retained.
5. Squeeze and release the handles to verify that the spring deforms and returns the jaws toward their open position.

No tools are required for assembly.

---

## Testing the Pliers

A successful design needed to do more than look like a pair of pliers. It needed to grip a small component and provide useful, repeatable motion.

### Functional Test: Through-Hole Resistors

![Pliers picking up a through-hole resistor](images/resistor-test.jpg)

The completed pliers were tested by gripping and picking up through-hole resistors.

The test demonstrates the primary purpose of the tool: the jaws can close around a small electronic component, maintain their grip during lifting, and reopen when the handles are released.

**Result: Successful.** The final pliers were able to grip and pick up through-hole resistors.

