## Beyond the Pliers: What Else Can 3D Printing Make Move?

One of the most interesting capabilities of additive manufacturing is the ability to create mechanisms that move without requiring conventional assembly. Print-in-place designs use carefully modeled clearances to allow separate components to move relative to one another after fabrication.

Instead of printing each component individually and assembling it with fasteners, the components can be printed in an already-connected configuration. This is particularly useful for hinges, gears, articulated structures, and other mechanisms with complex geometries.

Our pliers take a related but slightly different approach. Rather than printing the entire mechanism as one connected structure, we use separate rigid and flexible components to achieve the desired motion.

### Applications of Print-in-Place Technology

**1. Gears and mechanical transmissions**

[Explore print-in-place mechanisms and gears — All3DP](https://all3dp.com/)

3D-printed gear assemblies can incorporate multiple interacting components in a single build. Examples include planetary gear systems, gear trains, and mechanical demonstrations.

These mechanisms demonstrate how additive manufacturing can simplify assembly and create compact systems with moving components. The same design principles of clearance, alignment, and controlled motion are relevant to the joints and spring interfaces in our pliers.

**2. Articulated objects and mechanical joints**

[Print-in-place hinges and joints — OzFDM](https://ozfdm.com.au/blogs/knowledge-base/print-in-place-designs-hinges-joints-and-mechanisms)

Articulated models, chains, and hinges use connected geometries that remain mechanically separate after printing. They demonstrate how a design can incorporate motion without requiring individual components to be assembled afterward.

The key challenge is designing enough clearance to prevent adjacent surfaces from fusing during printing while maintaining sufficient precision for useful motion.

**3. Compliant mechanisms and robotic grippers**

[Compliant Mechanism Pliers — EduFacturing](https://edufacturing.com/en/projects-library/compliant-mechanism-pliers/)

Compliant mechanisms achieve motion through elastic deformation rather than relying entirely on conventional rotating joints. In the case of pliers, flexible sections can replace mechanical pivots or springs.

This is closely related to our design. Although our TPU spring is a separate component rather than an integrated flexure, both approaches use material deformation to produce motion.

Compliant mechanisms are also relevant to robotic grippers, where flexible structures can help accommodate objects of different shapes and distribute contact forces.

**4. Multi-material hinges and folding structures**

[Self-locking multi-material origami structures — Nature Communications](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-023-37343-w)

Researchers have demonstrated the use of rigid PLA structures combined with flexible TPU regions to create folding structures and flexible hinges.

This research illustrates how rigid and flexible materials can be assigned different mechanical roles within a single design. It also highlights the importance of the interface between the materials, particularly when the structure experiences repeated deformation.

Our design applies the same general principle through mechanically interlocking dovetail joints rather than relying on a permanently bonded multi-material print.

